/*
 * Nimbus Drop, protocol 3: the handshake and what hangs from it.
 *
 * What is modelled (Sources/NimbusCrypto unless said otherwise):
 *   Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256        Noise.write / Noise.read
 *   the prologue                        Noise.prologue:     "nimbus-drop/3" ‖ both openings
 *   Split with a third output           Noise.split: the two transport keys and `master`
 *   the hello                           SecureHello, the first sealed message each way: what the
 *                                       connection is for (SendSession.open, DropListener.serve)
 *   the keys of a drop                  DropKeys: session id, attach key, resume key, stream keys
 *   attaching a data connection         DropKeys.attachTag; ReceiveSession.claim (transfer core)
 *   a record on a data connection       RecordHeader.seal; ReceiveSession.receive
 *   resuming on a new handshake         DropKeys.resumeProof; ReceiveSession.resume
 *   a speed test                        SpeedTest, SpeedResponder: a drop's data path under the
 *                                       `master` of a connection whose hello said `speed`, and
 *                                       records coming back on connections of their own
 *
 * The attacker owns the network (Dolev-Yao), may run any number of Macs of its own, and may be
 * given any Mac's static key at any time (RevealStatic). Each lemma says which key must not
 * have been given away, and until when: everywhere it is the OTHER Mac's key, until the
 * handshake the lemma is about has finished at this Mac. This Mac's own key may be known from
 * the start (no key-compromise impersonation), and either key may be given away afterwards
 * (forward secrecy; and a drop that is running stays with the two Macs that began it).
 *
 * A KEY THAT IS NO MAC'S. The attacker can also make up a key pair, or present any value at
 * all as a static key. Nothing can be promised about a handshake with such a key (whoever
 * made it up knows everything Split gives), so every lemma about "the other Mac" is about a
 * key that some Mac made (Static). As first written the lemmas left that out and two were
 * false for that reason alone: a probe that reports the attacker's own made-up key, and a
 * `master` shared with it. In the code this is Trust's part: a key that proved itself is
 * still nobody until it is found paired.
 *
 * What is NOT modelled, and so not claimed:
 *   - ephemeral keys or session state leaking;
 *   - that the initiator's static key is hidden from anyone but the responder it expected
 *     (a privacy property; needs observational equivalence);
 *   - the limits on a key's use (2^24 seals, 2^38 bytes), the frame lengths, JSON;
 *   - AES-GCM, X25519, SHA-256 and HKDF themselves: they are ideal here. In particular a
 *     small-subgroup or non-canonical point is outside this model; PublicKey.init and
 *     KeyPair.agree refuse those in the code (TrustTests.testAKeyHasOneEncoding);
 *   - who may ask for what: that a key is paired, has not run out and was not retired is
 *     Trust's to say, and what happens when that changes is formal/tla/TrustWithdraw.tla;
 *   - the person: an offer accepted, a speed test allowed. Here a Mac takes whatever its
 *     paired peer's keys prove, which is more than the code lets through.
 *
 * The counter nonce of a CipherState is modelled as a public number chosen by the sender. A
 * connection carries one record here, so "the receiver takes each (key, number) at most
 * once", which is what a counter that only rises enforces (CipherState.open), is the same as
 * a connection number being taken once (Once, below).
 *
 * Opening a sealed value is written as a pattern: a rule takes aead(k, n, ad, p) only for the
 * k, n and ad it computes itself, which is what a tag that verifies means. (The first version
 * applied adec and a verify function to whatever arrived; the same thing, and the prover did
 * not finish.) The attacker still opens anything it has the key for (adec).
 *
 * An agreement that comes out as the neutral element is not refused here, though the code
 * refuses it (KeyPair.agree): the lemmas hold without that check.
 *
 * MUTANTS. Every guard the lemmas rest on is a macro (below, "The guards"), and each has a
 * flag that takes it out. With the flag, the lemma named beside it must come out falsified;
 * formal/check.py runs them and fails if one does not. A guard whose removal falsified
 * nothing was a lemma that said nothing about it: three were like that when the flags were
 * written (the prologue, the handshake hash in the resume proofs, a record's header as its
 * chunk's associated data), and each has a lemma now.
 * NOT SETTLED: NoConnInStreamKey, and with it NoHeaderBinding. With one stream key for a
 * whole drop the search was stopped at 12 GB in each of seven runs (two to three minutes
 * each), over the three lemmas that should notice. Nothing is claimed of those two flags:
 * they are not in check.py, and the lemmas are not shown to depend on those guards.
 *
 * HOW IT IS PROVED. The lemmas are in order and the later ones use the earlier ([reuse]):
 * first the handshake by itself, then two helpers saying that `master` names one handshake,
 * then the hello, then the drop. All of it is one theory and one run; nothing is assumed
 * between the parts. Three things made it finish:
 *   - A tactic (`drop`) that looks at the drop's own messages before the handshake's: the
 *     helpers already say what a finished handshake gives, so its messages need not be taken
 *     apart again.
 *   - Each helper ends "... or the other Mac's key was given away before this handshake
 *     finished". For the handshake a lemma is about (its time is always called #done) that
 *     is settled at once. For every other handshake in a trace it is an open question, and
 *     asking it of each of them doubled the search each time: with the lemmas allowing a key
 *     to be given away afterwards (as they did not at first: "never given away"), nothing
 *     about a drop finished inside 12 GB. So the tactic asks it only of #done, and leaves the
 *     rest until last.
 *   - A lemma seen from one end hides the helpers seen from the other (hide_lemma): they
 *     are true, and useless to it.
 * A tactic and a hidden helper order the search and take assumptions away; neither can make
 * a false lemma come out true.
 *
 * Run:  tamarin-prover --prove formal/tamarin/NimbusHandshake.spthy +RTS -N4 -RTS
 *       (the tactic prints the goals it ranks, which is long: the results are in the
 *       "summary of summaries" at the end)
 *       tamarin-prover --prove=a_probe_is_answered_by_the_key_it_reports -D=NoMessage2Check \
 *                      formal/tamarin/NimbusHandshake.spthy +RTS -N4 -RTS
 *       and so on for each flag. For a mutant ask for its own lemma only: the lemmas before
 *       it are assumed whether they were proved or not, and a mutant may have made one false.
 *       (A trace found is a trace all the same; "verified" under a mutant means nothing
 *       unless the helpers before the lemma still hold in it.)
 */
theory NimbusHandshake
begin

builtins: diffie-hellman, hashing

functions: aead/4, adec/3, hkdf1/2, hkdf2/2, hkdf3/2, kdf/2, mac/2

/* aead(key, nonce, associated data, plaintext). Opening needs the key, and the nonce and
   associated data that were used; nothing can be learned or checked without them. */
equations: adec(k, n, aead(k, n, ad, p)) = p

restriction Once:     "All x #i #j. Once(x) @ i & Once(x) @ j ==> #i = #j"

/* ---- The guards ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One macro for each thing the lemmas rest on, and beside it the line of the Swift it stands
   for. -D=<flag> swaps in the same thing without the guard; the lemma named must then fail.
   (Tamarin 1.12 takes #ifdef around a declaration, not inside a rule: hence macros.)        */

/* Both openings go into the handshake hash before anything else.
   Noise.init: mixHash(prologue), Crypto.swift:282; Noise.prologue, Version.swift:28.
   NoPrologue falsifies the_handshake_hash_binds_the_openings.                               */
#ifdef NoPrologue
macros: prologueHash(oi, orr) = h(<'Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256', 'nimbus-drop/3'>)
#else
macros: prologueHash(oi, orr) = h(<'Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256', 'nimbus-drop/3', oi, orr>)
#endif

/* The three agreements, each mixed into the chaining key.
   ee: Noise.write, Crypto.swift:324; Noise.read, :356. Without it nothing in the keys is
       new once both static keys are known: NoEE falsifies master_is_secret_for_the_initiator.
   es: :326 and :358. It is what proves the responder's key: NoES falsifies
       a_probe_is_answered_by_the_key_it_reports.
   se: :330 and :363. It is what proves the initiator's key: NoSE falsifies
       master_is_secret_for_the_responder.                                                    */
#ifdef NoEE
macros: dhEE(pub, sec) = 'none'
#else
macros: dhEE(pub, sec) = pub^sec
#endif

#ifdef NoES
macros: dhES(pub, sec) = 'none'
#else
macros: dhES(pub, sec) = pub^sec
#endif

#ifdef NoSE
macros: dhSE(pub, sec) = 'none'
#else
macros: dhSE(pub, sec) = pub^sec
#endif

/* Every seal of the handshake has the hash so far as its associated data.
   Noise.encryptAndHash, Crypto.swift:294; decryptAndHash, :301.
   NoHashInSeals falsifies the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator: the two ends finish with
   the same keys and different hashes (an opening altered on the way goes unnoticed).        */
#ifdef NoHashInSeals
macros: sealAD(hash) = 'none'
#else
macros: sealAD(hash) = hash
#endif

/* The initiator opens the last seal of message 2, which is under the key that has `es` in
   it. Noise.read, Crypto.swift:366 at step 1 (the tag is AES.GCM.open's to check, :207).
   NoMessage2Check falsifies a_probe_is_answered_by_the_key_it_reports.                      */
#ifdef NoMessage2Check
macros: message2Payload(key, ad, whatever) = whatever
#else
macros: message2Payload(key, ad, whatever) = aead(key, '0', ad, 'empty')
#endif

/* The responder opens the last seal of message 3, which is under the key that has `se` in
   it. Noise.read, Crypto.swift:366 at step 2.
   NoMessage3Check falsifies the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator.                        */
#ifdef NoMessage3Check
macros: message3Payload(key, ad, whatever) = whatever
#else
macros: message3Payload(key, ad, whatever) = aead(key, '0', ad, 'empty')
#endif

/* Split gives a key for each direction. Noise.split, Crypto.swift:377.
   OneTransportKey falsifies the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder: its own hello comes back
   to it as the responder's.                                                                  */
#ifdef OneTransportKey
macros: keyRI(ck) = hkdf1(ck, 'split')
#else
macros: keyRI(ck) = hkdf2(ck, 'split')
#endif

/* `master` is the third output of the same HKDF, keyed by the chaining key.
   Noise.split, Crypto.swift:375. MasterFromHash takes it from the handshake hash instead,
   which anyone watching can compute: it falsifies master_is_secret_for_the_initiator.       */
#ifdef MasterFromHash
macros: masterOf(ck, hash) = hkdf3(hash, 'split')
#else
macros: masterOf(ck, hash) = hkdf3(ck, 'split')
#endif

/* What a connection is for is said inside the channel. Channel.send, Channel.swift:225;
   Channel.read, :233; DropListener.serve reads it there, Listener.swift:269.
   PurposeInTheClear falsifies both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for.                     */
#ifdef PurposeInTheClear
macros: purposeHello(key, body) = body
#else
macros: purposeHello(key, body) = aead(key, '0', 'none', body)
#endif

/* The attach key has a label of its own. DropKeys.init, DropKeys.swift:23 (the session id,
   which crosses the network in the clear, is :22).
   AttachKeyIsSessionID falsifies only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection.                */
#ifdef AttachKeyIsSessionID
macros: attachKey(master) = kdf(master, 'session id')
#else
macros: attachKey(master) = kdf(master, 'attach')
#endif

/* The tag is over the session id, the connection's number and the receiver's challenge.
   DropKeys.attachTag, DropKeys.swift:33; the challenge is DropListener.serve's,
   Listener.swift:199. NoChallengeInTag and NoConnInTag each falsify
   only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection. (The session id in it is a second tie and
   not the only one: the attach key is this drop's alone. Taken out, nothing fails.)         */
#ifdef NoChallengeInTag
macros: attachTag(akey, sid, conn, c) = mac(akey, <sid, conn>)
#endif
#ifdef NoConnInTag
macros: attachTag(akey, sid, conn, c) = mac(akey, <sid, c>)
#endif
#ifdef not NoChallengeInTag & not NoConnInTag
macros: attachTag(akey, sid, conn, c) = mac(akey, <sid, conn, c>)
#endif

/* Each data connection has a key of its own. DropKeys.stream, DropKeys.swift:29.
   NoConnInStreamKey should falsify what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed (a record moves
   to another connection of the drop) and what_a_speed_test_counts_is_what_the_other_mac_sealed
   (a record the tester sent comes back to it). NOT SETTLED: the search did not finish.      */
#ifdef NoConnInStreamKey
macros: streamKey(master, conn) = kdf(master, 'stream')
#else
macros: streamKey(master, conn) = kdf(master, <'stream', conn>)
#endif

/* A chunk is sealed with its header as associated data. RecordHeader.seal,
   DropKeys.swift:59; ReceiveSession.receive, Receiver.swift:516.
   By itself NoHeaderBinding should falsify nothing, here or in the code: a header and its
   chunk are consecutive counts under a key nothing else is sealed with, and that ties them
   already. The header as associated data is the tie that is left when a stream key is used
   on two connections: with NoConnInStreamKey, a_chunk_is_written_only_under_its_own_header
   should still hold, and with both flags be falsified. NOT SETTLED, any of the three: the
   search did not finish (see MUTANTS above).                                                */
#ifdef NoHeaderBinding
macros: chunkAD(hdr) = 'none'
#else
macros: chunkAD(hdr) = hdr
#endif

/* The two proofs of a resume are over the new handshake's hash, each under its own label.
   DropKeys.resumeProof, DropKeys.swift:36; SendSession.reconnect, Sender.swift:272 and :275;
   ReceiveSession.resume, Receiver.swift:157 and :158.
   NoHashInResume falsifies only_the_sender_resumes_a_drop: a proof made for one handshake
   is good for another. SameResumeLabel falsifies only_the_receiver_confirms_a_resume: the
   sender's own proof is handed back to it as the receiver's.                                */
#ifdef NoHashInResume
macros: resumeProof(rkey, hash) = mac(rkey, 'resume'), resumedProof(rkey, hash) = mac(rkey, 'resumed')
#endif
#ifdef SameResumeLabel
macros: resumeProof(rkey, hash) = mac(rkey, <'resume', hash>), resumedProof(rkey, hash) = mac(rkey, <'resume', hash>)
#endif
#ifdef not NoHashInResume & not SameResumeLabel
macros: resumeProof(rkey, hash) = mac(rkey, <'resume', hash>), resumedProof(rkey, hash) = mac(rkey, <'resumed', hash>)
#endif

/* ---- Macs and their static keys ---------------------------------------------------------- */

rule Make_Static:
    [ Fr(~s) ]
  --[ Static($A, 'g'^~s), SecretOf('g'^~s, ~s), Once(<'static', $A>) ]->
    [ !Ltk($A, ~s), !Pk($A, 'g'^~s), Out('g'^~s) ]

rule Reveal_Static:
    [ !Ltk($A, ~s) ]
  --[ RevealStatic('g'^~s) ]->
    [ Out(~s) ]

/* ---- The handshake ------------------------------------------------------------------------
   -> e
   <- e, ee, s, es
   -> s, se
   The openings ($oi, $or: "NDROP" and the majors each side speaks) cross in the clear first and
   are mixed in as the prologue. An attacker may alter them on the way: each side hashes what it
   sent and what it received.

   A handshake message carries no payload (Channel.respond and Channel.begin refuse one); the
   empty payload is still sealed and hashed, as Noise does.                                   */

rule I_1:
  let gEI = 'g'^~ei
      h0  = prologueHash($oi, orr)
      h1  = h(<h0, gEI>)
      h1p = h(<h1, 'empty'>)
  in
    [ Fr(~ei), !Ltk($I, ~si), In(orr) ]
  --[ IStart($I, gEI), Eph(~ei) ]->
    [ I_St1($I, ~si, ~ei, h1p, $oi, orr), Out(<$oi, gEI>) ]

rule R_1:
  let gER = 'g'^~er
      gSR = 'g'^~sr
      h0  = prologueHash(oi, $or)
      h1  = h(<h0, gEI>)
      h1p = h(<h1, 'empty'>)
      h2  = h(<h1p, gER>)
      ee  = dhEE(gEI, ~er)
      ck1 = hkdf1('Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256', ee)
      k1  = hkdf2('Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256', ee)
      cs  = aead(k1, '0', sealAD(h2), gSR)
      h3  = h(<h2, cs>)
      es  = dhES(gEI, ~sr)
      ck2 = hkdf1(ck1, es)
      k2  = hkdf2(ck1, es)
      cp  = aead(k2, '0', sealAD(h3), 'empty')
      h4  = h(<h3, cp>)
  in
    [ Fr(~er), !Ltk($R, ~sr), In(<oi, gEI>) ]
  --[ RAnswered($R, gSR, gEI), Eph(~er) ]->
    [ R_St1($R, ~sr, ~er, gEI, ck2, k2, h4, oi, $or), Out(<gER, cs, cp>) ]

/* The initiator after message 2: the responder's key is read and proved; nothing of this Mac
   has been said. This is where a probe stops (Channel.probe) and where `expect` and `check`
   are applied (Channel.initiate).                                                            */
rule I_2:
  let gEI = 'g'^~ei
      h2  = h(<h1p, gER>)
      ee  = dhEE(gER, ~ei)
      ck1 = hkdf1('Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256', ee)
      k1  = hkdf2('Noise_XX_25519_AESGCM_SHA256', ee)
      cs  = aead(k1, '0', sealAD(h2), gSR)
      h3  = h(<h2, cs>)
      es  = dhES(gSR, ~ei)
      ck2 = hkdf1(ck1, es)
      k2  = hkdf2(ck1, es)
      cp  = message2Payload(k2, sealAD(h3), whatever)
      h4  = h(<h3, cp>)
  in
    [ I_St1($I, ~si, ~ei, h1p, oi, orr), In(<gER, cs, cp>) ]
  --[ Probed($I, gSR, gEI) ]->
    [ I_St2($I, ~si, gER, gSR, ck2, k2, h4, oi, orr) ]

/* Message 3: only now is this Mac's key said, sealed for the responder that was proved.     */
rule I_3:
  let gSI = 'g'^~si
      cs2 = aead(k2, '1', sealAD(h4), gSI)
      h5  = h(<h4, cs2>)
      se  = dhSE(gER, ~si)
      ck3 = hkdf1(ck2, se)
      k3  = hkdf2(ck2, se)
      cp2 = aead(k3, '0', sealAD(h5), 'empty')
      h6  = h(<h5, cp2>)
      kIR = hkdf1(ck3, 'split')
      kRI = keyRI(ck3)
      master = masterOf(ck3, h6)
  in
    [ I_St2($I, ~si, gER, gSR, ck2, k2, h4, oi, orr) ]
  --[ IDone($I, gSI, gSR, h6, kIR, kRI, master),
      Openings('I', h6, oi, orr) ]->
    [ Out(<cs2, cp2>),
      I_Open(gSI, gSR, h6, kIR, kRI, master) ]

rule R_2:
  let gSR = 'g'^~sr
      cs2 = aead(k2, '1', sealAD(h4), gSI)
      h5  = h(<h4, cs2>)
      se  = dhSE(gSI, ~er)
      ck3 = hkdf1(ck2, se)
      k3  = hkdf2(ck2, se)
      cp2 = message3Payload(k3, sealAD(h5), whatever)
      h6  = h(<h5, cp2>)
      kIR = hkdf1(ck3, 'split')
      kRI = keyRI(ck3)
      master = masterOf(ck3, h6)
  in
    [ R_St1($R, ~sr, ~er, gEI, ck2, k2, h4, oi, orr), In(<cs2, cp2>) ]
  --[ RDone($R, gSR, gSI, h6, kIR, kRI, master),
      Openings('R', h6, oi, orr) ]->
    [ R_Open(gSR, gSI, h6, kIR, kRI, master) ]

/* ---- The hello: the first sealed message each way ------------------------------------------
   The initiator says what the connection is for ($p: drop, resume, speed, pair, verify,
   retire, or nothing; with it, in the code, its name, its key's time left and whether it has
   this Mac paired), and the responder answers with its own hello ($x). Only then is the
   connection anything: a channel is used for one purpose, the one its hello gave
   (SendSession.open, SpeedTest.run; DropListener.serve switches on it once and returns).
   The channel's state before that is a fact that is used up, so each end says and hears one
   hello for a handshake: what a CipherState's count does (count 0 is sealed once).          */

rule I_Hello:
  let hello = purposeHello(kIR, <'hello', $p>)
  in
    [ I_Open(gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master) ]
  --[ ISaid(gSI, gSR, hh, master, $p) ]->
    [ Out(hello), I_Wait($p, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master) ]

rule R_Hello:
  let hello = purposeHello(kIR, <'hello', p>)
  in
    [ R_Open(gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, master), In(hello) ]
  --[ RHeard(gSR, gSI, hh, master, p), RSaid(gSR, gSI, hh, master, $x) ]->
    [ Out(aead(kRI, '0', 'none', <'hello', $x>)), !Sess('R', p, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, master) ]

rule I_Hears:
    [ I_Wait(p, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master), In(aead(kRI, '0', 'none', <'hello', x>)) ]
  --[ IHeard(gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master, p, x) ]->
    [ !Sess('I', p, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master) ]

/* ---- Data connections: one master, from the handshake that carried the offer ---------------
   The initiator of that handshake is the sender. The receiver sends a fresh challenge; the
   sender answers with the session id, a connection number and a tag under the attach key
   (ReceiveSession.claim), and then seals records under that connection's key
   (ReceiveSession.receive). A secret chunk goes in the one record a connection carries here.

   Each connection number is used once under a master, at both ends (Once): the sender takes
   numbers from one counter for the whole drop (SendSession.attach, Sender.swift:488), and the
   receiver takes each number once (ReceiveSession.claim, Receiver.swift:110;
   SpeedResponder.claim, SpeedTest.swift:347).

   The rules are the same whatever the hello said the connection was for (p), and p is in
   every fact: a speed test's first half is exactly this, with the bytes thrown away instead
   of written (SpeedWire.receive), so "Written" for p = 'speed' reads "opened and counted".
   That a drop's rules can also run under some other purpose is more than the code does, and
   the lemmas hold all the same.                                                              */

rule Receiver_Challenge:
    [ !Sess('R', p, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, master), Fr(~c) ]
  --[ Challenge(master, ~c) ]->
    [ Data_R(p, gSR, gSI, hh, master, ~c), Out(~c) ]

rule Sender_Attach:
  let sid  = kdf(master, 'session id')
      akey = attachKey(master)
      skey = streamKey(master, $conn)
      tag  = attachTag(akey, sid, $conn, c)
      hdr  = <$file, $index>
      rec  = <aead(skey, <'hdr', $n>, 'none', hdr), aead(skey, <'chunk', $n>, chunkAD(hdr), ~chunk)>
  in
    [ !Sess('I', p, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master), In(c), Fr(~chunk) ]
  --[ Attached(p, gSI, gSR, hh, master, $conn, c),
      Sealed(p, gSI, gSR, hh, master, $conn, $n, hdr, ~chunk),
      SecretChunk(gSI, gSR, hh, ~chunk),
      Once(<'send conn', master, $conn>) ]->
    [ Out(<<sid, $conn, tag>, <$n, rec>>) ]

/* Taking a connection and reading from it are two steps, as in the code: the tag is checked
   before any record is looked at. (They were one rule at first, and then a tag that did not
   cover the connection's number went unnoticed: the record's key covered for it.)           */
rule Receiver_Claims:
  let sid  = kdf(master, 'session id')
      akey = attachKey(master)
      tag  = attachTag(akey, sid, conn, ~c)
  in
    [ Data_R(p, gSR, gSI, hh, master, ~c), In(<sid, conn, tag>) ]
  --[ Claimed(p, gSR, gSI, hh, master, conn, ~c),
      Once(<'claim conn', master, conn>) ]->
    [ Stream_R(p, gSR, gSI, hh, master, conn) ]

rule Receiver_Takes_Record:
  let skey = streamKey(master, conn)
      rec  = <aead(skey, <'hdr', n>, 'none', hdr), aead(skey, <'chunk', n>, chunkAD(hdr), body)>
  in
    [ Stream_R(p, gSR, gSI, hh, master, conn), In(<n, rec>) ]
  --[ Written(p, gSR, gSI, hh, master, conn, n, hdr, body) ]->
    [ ]

/* ---- A speed test's second half: records coming back ---------------------------------------
   The tester opens and proves every connection of both directions (SpeedTest.attach). On the
   first n it sends, which is the rules above with p = 'speed'. On the rest the Mac that was
   asked sends and the tester opens and counts (SpeedResponder.carry, SpeedTest.swift:360;
   SpeedWire.receive). A connection goes one way only: at the tester because a number is
   used once (Once, 'send conn'), at the other Mac because a connection it took is a fact
   that is used up by reading from it or by writing to it.                                    */

rule Tester_Attach_Down:
  let sid  = kdf(master, 'session id')
      akey = attachKey(master)
      tag  = attachTag(akey, sid, $conn, c)
  in
    [ !Sess('I', 'speed', gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master), In(c) ]
  --[ Attached('speed', gSI, gSR, hh, master, $conn, c),
      Once(<'send conn', master, $conn>) ]->
    [ Out(<sid, $conn, tag>), Down_I(gSI, gSR, hh, master, $conn) ]

rule Responder_Sends_Down:
  let skey = streamKey(master, conn)
      hdr  = <$file, $index>
      rec  = <aead(skey, <'hdr', $n>, 'none', hdr), aead(skey, <'chunk', $n>, chunkAD(hdr), ~bytes)>
  in
    [ Stream_R('speed', gSR, gSI, hh, master, conn), Fr(~bytes) ]
  --[ SealedDown(gSR, gSI, hh, master, conn, $n, hdr, ~bytes) ]->
    [ Out(<$n, rec>) ]

rule Tester_Counts_Down:
  let skey = streamKey(master, conn)
      rec  = <aead(skey, <'hdr', n>, 'none', hdr), aead(skey, <'chunk', n>, chunkAD(hdr), bytes)>
  in
    [ Down_I(gSI, gSR, hh, master, conn), In(<n, rec>) ]
  --[ CountedDown(gSI, gSR, hh, master, conn, n, hdr, bytes) ]->
    [ ]

/* ---- Resume: a new handshake between the same two keys, and proof of the drop's keys -------
   The new connection's hello says `resume`; the drop's own said `drop`. Both ends ask for
   the same key at the other end as before (SendSession.reconnect: `expect: peer`;
   DropListener.serve: `session.peerKey == key`), and the proofs go as the channel's second
   message each way.                                                                          */

rule Sender_Resume:
  let rkey  = kdf(master, 'resume')
      sid   = kdf(master, 'session id')
      proof = resumeProof(rkey, h2)
  in
    [ !Sess('I', 'drop', gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, master),
      !Sess('I', 'resume', gSI, gSR, h2, kIR2, kRI2, master2) ]
  --[ ResumeAsked(gSI, gSR, hh, master, h2) ]->
    [ Out(aead(kIR2, '1', 'none', <'resume', sid, proof>)),
      Resuming(gSI, gSR, hh, master, h2, kRI2) ]

rule Receiver_Resumes:
  let rkey  = kdf(master, 'resume')
      sid   = kdf(master, 'session id')
      proof = resumeProof(rkey, h2)
      back  = resumedProof(rkey, h2)
  in
    [ !Sess('R', 'drop', gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, master),
      !Sess('R', 'resume', gSR, gSI, h2, kIR2, kRI2, master2),
      In(aead(kIR2, '1', 'none', <'resume', sid, proof>)) ]
  --[ Resumed(gSR, gSI, hh, master, h2) ]->
    [ Out(aead(kRI2, '1', 'none', <'resumed', back>)) ]

rule Sender_Resumed:
  let rkey  = kdf(master, 'resume')
      back  = resumedProof(rkey, h2)
  in
    [ Resuming(gSI, gSR, hh, master, h2, kRI2), In(aead(kRI2, '1', 'none', <'resumed', back>)) ]
  --[ ResumeConfirmed(gSI, gSR, hh, master, h2) ]->
    [ ]

/* ================================== Lemmas ================================================ */

/* The model can run: every honest flow, to its end, with no key given away. Without these a
   lemma could hold because nothing ever happens.                                             */
lemma can_finish_a_handshake: exists-trace
  "Ex I R gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m #i #j.
      IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ j
      & not (Ex X #r. RevealStatic(X) @ r)"

/* A probe: the responder's key proved, and the prober hangs up without saying its own.       */
lemma can_probe_and_hang_up: exists-trace
  "Ex I R gSR gEI #i #j.
      Probed(I, gSR, gEI) @ i & RAnswered(R, gSR, gEI) @ j
      & not (Ex I2 gSI gSR2 hh kIR kRI m #k. IDone(I2, gSI, gSR2, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ k)
      & not (Ex X #r. RevealStatic(X) @ r)"

lemma can_say_what_a_connection_is_for: exists-trace
  "Ex gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m p x #i #j.
      IHeard(gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m, p, x) @ i & RHeard(gSR, gSI, hh, m, p) @ j
      & not (Ex X #r. RevealStatic(X) @ r)"

lemma can_deliver_a_record: exists-trace
  "Ex gSR gSI hh m conn n hdr body #i #j.
      Written('drop', gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ i & Sealed('drop', gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ j
      & not (Ex X #r. RevealStatic(X) @ r)"

lemma can_resume: exists-trace
  "Ex gSI gSR hh m h2 #i. ResumeConfirmed(gSI, gSR, hh, m, h2) @ i & not (Ex X #r. RevealStatic(X) @ r)"

/* A speed test: a record each way, on two connections of the one test.                       */
lemma can_run_a_speed_test: exists-trace
  "Ex gSR gSI hh m c1 n1 hdr1 b1 c2 n2 hdr2 b2 #i #j #k.
      Written('speed', gSR, gSI, hh, m, c1, n1, hdr1, b1) @ i
      & SealedDown(gSR, gSI, hh, m, c2, n2, hdr2, b2) @ j & CountedDown(gSI, gSR, hh, m, c2, n2, hdr2, b2) @ k
      & not (Ex X #r. RevealStatic(X) @ r)"

/* And the case the lemmas below make a point of covering can happen: a handshake between two
   Macs, finished by both, when the initiator's own key was given away before it began.       */
lemma can_finish_when_the_initiators_own_key_was_given_away: exists-trace
  "Ex I R gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m #i #j #r.
      IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ j
      & RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #i & #r < #j
      & not (Ex #r2. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r2)"

/* Helpers ([reuse]: proved here, then used by the lemmas below). A Mac's ephemeral secret is
   never said; its static secret is known only by being given away.                            */
lemma an_ephemeral_secret_is_never_known [reuse]:
  "All e #i #j. Eph(e) @ i & KU(e) @ j ==> F"

lemma a_static_secret_is_known_only_by_being_given_away [reuse]:
  "All key s #i #j. SecretOf(key, s) @ i & KU(s) @ j ==> Ex #r. RevealStatic(key) @ r & #r < #j"

/* A probe's answer: the key it returns was proved, for this probe's own ephemeral, by a Mac
   that holds it. (What that key is to this Mac is Trust's to say.)                           */
lemma a_probe_is_answered_by_the_key_it_reports:
  "All I B gSR gEI #i #b. Probed(I, gSR, gEI) @ i & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> (Ex R #j. RAnswered(R, gSR, gEI) @ j & #j < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #i)"

/* Secrecy of everything Split gives, as the initiator sees it and as the responder sees it:
   unless the OTHER Mac's static key was given away before the handshake finished. This Mac's
   own key being known does not help (no key-compromise impersonation), and either key being
   given away afterwards does not either (forward secrecy). KU: the attacker cannot work it
   out at all, which is what lets the lemmas further down use these.                          */
lemma master_is_secret_for_the_initiator [reuse]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m #i #b. IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> not (Ex #k. KU(m) @ k) | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #i)"

lemma master_is_secret_for_the_responder [reuse]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m #i #a. RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> not (Ex #k. KU(m) @ k) | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #i)"

lemma transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator [reuse]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m #i #b. IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> (not (Ex #k. KU(kIR) @ k) & not (Ex #k. KU(kRI) @ k)) | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #i)"

lemma transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder [reuse]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m #i #a. RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (not (Ex #k. KU(kIR) @ k) & not (Ex #k. KU(kRI) @ k)) | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #i)"

/* Agreement. When the responder finishes with an initiator whose key was not given away, that
   initiator finished the same handshake: same two keys, same hash (and so the same openings:
   nobody chose the protocol major for them), same keys out of Split. Exactly one such
   handshake (injective): a finished handshake cannot be replayed into a second.              */
lemma the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator [reuse]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m #i #a. RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (Ex I #j. IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ j & #j < #i
             & not (Ex R2 #i2. RDone(R2, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i2 & not (#i2 = #i)))
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #i)"

/* The initiator finishes first, so what it can know then is that the responder it names
   answered this very handshake. That the responder went on to the same keys it learns from
   the responder's first sealed message: the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder, below.       */
lemma the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder [reuse]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m #i #b. IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> (Ex R gEI #j. RAnswered(R, gSR, gEI) @ j & #j < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #i)"

/* One handshake hash, one pair of keys: two Macs that hold the same `h` each hold the other's
   real static key. No condition on who knows what. This is what lets the pairing's codes,
   which are computed from `h`, stand for the keys (NimbusPairing.spthy assumes exactly this).*/
lemma the_handshake_hash_binds_both_static_keys [reuse]:
  "All I R gSI gSRseen gSR gSIseen hh k1 k2 m k3 k4 m2 #i #j.
      IDone(I, gSI, gSRseen, hh, k1, k2, m) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSIseen, hh, k3, k4, m2) @ j
      ==> gSRseen = gSR & gSIseen = gSI & m = m2"

/* And one pair of openings: two Macs that hold the same `h` each saw the list of majors the
   other sent. No condition. With the agreement above: nobody altered an opening on the way
   to a handshake that finished. (Until this lemma was written, taking the prologue out of
   the hash falsified nothing.)                                                               */
lemma the_handshake_hash_binds_the_openings:
  "All hh oi orr oi2 or2 #i #j. Openings('I', hh, oi, orr) @ i & Openings('R', hh, oi2, or2) @ j
      ==> oi = oi2 & orr = or2"

/* A hash names one handshake at each end: neither end finishes two with the same `h`. No
   condition. With the two agreements, each end's handshake answers to exactly one of the
   other's.                                                                                   */
lemma a_handshake_hash_is_one_initiators:
  "All I gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m I2 gSI2 gSR2 kIR2 kRI2 m2 #i #j.
      IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & IDone(I2, gSI2, gSR2, hh, kIR2, kRI2, m2) @ j ==> #i = #j"

lemma a_handshake_hash_is_one_responders:
  "All R gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m R2 gSR2 gSI2 kIR2 kRI2 m2 #i #j.
      RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & RDone(R2, gSR2, gSI2, hh, kIR2, kRI2, m2) @ j ==> #i = #j"

/* Helpers for what follows: `master` names one handshake. When a responder finished with a
   Mac whose key had not been given away by then, any initiator that holds the same `master`
   is the one it finished with (same keys, same hash, same transport keys); and the same from
   the initiator's end. This is what keeps one drop's keys, or a speed test's, from being
   anything to another. (With no condition at all, that no two handshakes ever share a
   `master`, the prover was stopped after a minute and 5 GB; that stronger statement is not
   proved here.)                                                                              */
lemma the_responders_master_is_one_handshake [reuse]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI I2 gSI2 gSR2 hh2 kIR2 kRI2 m #i #j #a.
      RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      & IDone(I2, gSI2, gSR2, hh2, kIR2, kRI2, m) @ j
      ==> (gSI2 = gSI & gSR2 = gSR & hh2 = hh & kIR2 = kIR & kRI2 = kRI)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #i)"

lemma the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake [reuse, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI R2 gSR2 gSI2 hh2 kIR2 kRI2 m #i #j #b.
      IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      & RDone(R2, gSR2, gSI2, hh2, kIR2, kRI2, m) @ j
      ==> (gSI2 = gSI & gSR2 = gSR & hh2 = hh & kIR2 = kIR & kRI2 = kRI)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #i)"

/* And at each end `master` is no other handshake's of that end: two responders never hold
   the same one, nor two initiators (when the first finished with a key not given away by
   then). Without these a guard whose removal lets two connections share a key sends the
   search past 12 GB: it has to rule out, from the handshake's own messages, that an end
   finished twice with one `master`. With them, OneTransportKey is found in 24 s.            */
lemma the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders [reuse, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI R2 gSR2 gSI2 hh2 kIR2 kRI2 m #i #j #a.
      RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      & RDone(R2, gSR2, gSI2, hh2, kIR2, kRI2, m) @ j
      ==> #i = #j
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #i)"

lemma the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators [reuse, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI I2 gSI2 gSR2 hh2 kIR2 kRI2 m #i #j #b.
      IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ i & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      & IDone(I2, gSI2, gSR2, hh2, kIR2, kRI2, m) @ j
      ==> #i = #j
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #i)"

/* How the lemmas from here on are searched (see HOW IT IS PROVED): the facts of the drop
   first; then what the helpers say of the lemma's own handshake, whose time every lemma
   below calls #done; then tags, keys and records derived from `master`; the handshake's own
   messages last, and last of all what the helpers say of any other handshake.                */
tactic: drop
presort: C
prio:
    regex ".*Data_R\(.*" | regex ".*Stream_R\(.*" | regex ".*Down_I\(.*" | regex ".*!Sess\(.*" | regex ".*Resuming\(.*" | regex ".*I_Wait\(.*" | regex ".*I_Open\(.*" | regex ".*R_Open\(.*"
prio:
    regex "^ +\(.*< #done\)"
prio:
    regex ".*!KU\( mac\(.*" | regex ".*!KU\( kdf\(.*" | regex ".*!KU\( aead\(kdf\(.*" | regex ".*!KU\( ~.*"
prio:
    regex ".*!KU\( aead\(hkdf[12]\(hkdf1\(hkdf1\(hkdf1\(.*" | regex ".*!KU\( hkdf[123]\(hkdf1\(hkdf1\(hkdf1\(.*"
deprio:
    regex ".*!KU\( h\(.*" | regex ".*!KU\( aead\(hkdf2\(.*" | regex ".*!KU\( hkdf.*" | regex ".*!KU\( 'g'.*" | regex ".*St1\(.*" | regex ".*splitEqs.*" | regex ".*∥.*"

/* ---- The hello ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ */

/* What the responder takes a connection to be for is what the initiator said it was for, on
   this handshake. So a Mac that opened a connection for a speed test has not offered a drop,
   and one that came to resume has not asked for anything else.                               */
lemma both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for [reuse, heuristic={drop}]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m p #i #done #a.
      RHeard(gSR, gSI, hh, m, p) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (Ex #k. ISaid(gSI, gSR, hh, m, p) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* The hello the initiator reads was said by the responder it proved, which by then had
   finished this same handshake: same keys, same hash, same `master`. It is not the
   initiator's own hello come back (the two directions have different keys), and with it the
   initiator knows what the responder knew at the end of message 3.                           */
lemma the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder [reuse, heuristic={drop}]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m p x #i #done #b.
      IHeard(gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m, p, x) @ i & IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> (Ex R #k #l. RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ k & #k < #l
             & RSaid(gSR, gSI, hh, m, x) @ l & #l < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* ---- Data connections ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* A data connection is taken only from the Mac that holds the drop's keys, for that
   connection number, for the challenge this receiver made for it, and for a connection of
   the same purpose. Also when the sender's key is given away while the drop runs: it is the
   drop's keys that attach, not the Mac's.                                                    */
lemma only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection [reuse, heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All p R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m conn c #i #done #a.
      Claimed(p, gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, c) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (Ex #k. Attached(p, gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn, c) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* What the header as associated data is for: a chunk is written only under the header
   (which file, which chunk) the sender sealed it with. Here it follows from the lemma below
   and is proved before it, so that a mutant of that one cannot be assumed; that it holds by
   the header alone, with one stream key for a whole drop, is NOT SETTLED (see MUTANTS).     */
lemma a_chunk_is_written_only_under_its_own_header [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All p R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body #i #done #a.
      Written(p, gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (Ex conn2 n2 #k. Sealed(p, gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn2, n2, hdr, body) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* The one outcome that must never happen, as far as the cryptography goes: what is written
   is what the sender sealed, under that header, on that connection, at that count, for a
   connection of that purpose.                                                                */
lemma what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed [reuse, heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All p R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body #i #done #a.
      Written(p, gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (Ex #k. Sealed(p, gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* The other direction of a speed test: what the tester opens and counts was sealed by the
   Mac it asked, for this test, on that connection. In particular it is not what the tester
   itself sent on another connection.                                                         */
lemma what_a_speed_test_counts_is_what_the_other_mac_sealed [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders, hide_lemma=both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for, hide_lemma=only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection, hide_lemma=what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr bytes #i #done #b.
      CountedDown(gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, bytes) @ i & IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> (Ex #k. SealedDown(gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, bytes) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* A speed test and a drop: nothing of one is taken by the other. Whatever was sealed with
   the bytes that got written was sealed for a connection of the same purpose, on the same
   handshake, under the same `master`; and nothing a speed test sends back is ever written.
   (An attach is covered by only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection, which has the purpose
   in it; a resume is of a connection whose hello said `drop`, by the rules.)                 */
lemma what_is_sealed_for_one_purpose_is_never_written_for_another [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All p q R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body gSI2 gSR2 hh2 m2 conn2 n2 hdr2 #i #done #a #k.
      Written(p, gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      & Sealed(q, gSI2, gSR2, hh2, m2, conn2, n2, hdr2, body) @ k
      ==> (q = p & hh2 = hh & m2 = m)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

lemma what_a_speed_test_sends_back_is_never_written [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All p R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body gSI2 gSR2 hh2 m2 conn2 n2 hdr2 #i #done #a #k.
      Written(p, gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      & SealedDown(gSR2, gSI2, hh2, m2, conn2, n2, hdr2, body) @ k
      ==> (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* Replay. A chunk the sender sealed is written only where it was sealed for (that Mac, that
   handshake, that connection, that count, that header), whoever else the receiver, or any
   other Mac, is talking to; and so it is written at most once. As the sender sees it: its
   own key may be known.                                                                      */
lemma a_sealed_chunk_is_written_only_where_it_was_sealed_for [reuse, heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders, hide_lemma=both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for, hide_lemma=only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection, hide_lemma=what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed]:
  "All p I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body p2 gSR2 gSI2 hh2 m2 conn2 n2 hdr2 #s #done #b #k.
      Sealed(p, gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ s & IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      & Written(p2, gSR2, gSI2, hh2, m2, conn2, n2, hdr2, body) @ k
      ==> (gSR2 = gSR & gSI2 = gSI & hh2 = hh & m2 = m & conn2 = conn & n2 = n & hdr2 = hdr)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #done)"

lemma a_sealed_chunk_is_written_once [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders, hide_lemma=both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for, hide_lemma=only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection, hide_lemma=what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed]:
  "All p I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body p2 gSR2 gSI2 hh2 m2 conn2 n2 hdr2 p3 gSR3 gSI3 hh3 m3 conn3 n3 hdr3 #s #done #b #i #k.
      Sealed(p, gSI, gSR, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ s & IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      & Written(p2, gSR2, gSI2, hh2, m2, conn2, n2, hdr2, body) @ i
      & Written(p3, gSR3, gSI3, hh3, m3, conn3, n3, hdr3, body) @ k
      ==> #i = #k
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* A chunk is secret, as the sender sees it: unless the receiver's key was given away before
   the drop's handshake finished. Not if it is given away later, and not if the sender's is.  */
lemma a_chunk_is_secret [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders, hide_lemma=both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for, hide_lemma=only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection, hide_lemma=what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m chunk #i #done #b.
      SecretChunk(gSI, gSR, hh, chunk) @ i & IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> not (Ex #k. K(chunk) @ k)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* And as the receiver sees it: what it wrote is secret, unless the sender's key was given
   away before the drop's handshake finished.                                                 */
lemma what_is_written_is_secret [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All p R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m conn n hdr body #i #done #a.
      Written(p, gSR, gSI, hh, m, conn, n, hdr, body) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> not (Ex #k. K(body) @ k)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

/* ---- Resume --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* A drop is picked up again only by the Mac that holds its keys, on the handshake the proof
   was made for; and the sender takes "resumed" only from the receiver of that drop, for that
   handshake. In both, the key that must not have been given away is the other Mac's, and
   only until the DROP's handshake finished: a key given away after that (either Mac's, or
   both) lets someone finish a new handshake under it, and that is not enough to pick the
   drop up or to answer for it. (As first written these two allowed no key to be given away
   at any time, and then they said nothing of the hash in the proofs: the channel alone
   carried them.)                                                                             */
lemma only_the_sender_resumes_a_drop [reuse, heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_initiator_agrees_with_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_initiators_master_is_no_other_initiators, hide_lemma=the_initiator_hears_only_the_responder]:
  "All R A gSR gSI hh kIR kRI m h2 #i #done #a.
      Resumed(gSR, gSI, hh, m, h2) @ i & RDone(R, gSR, gSI, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(A, gSI) @ a
      ==> (Ex #k. ResumeAsked(gSI, gSR, hh, m, h2) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSI) @ r & #r < #done)"

lemma only_the_receiver_confirms_a_resume [heuristic={drop}, hide_lemma=master_is_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=transport_keys_are_secret_for_the_responder, hide_lemma=the_responder_agrees_with_the_initiator, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_one_handshake, hide_lemma=the_responders_master_is_no_other_responders, hide_lemma=both_ends_agree_what_a_connection_is_for, hide_lemma=only_the_sender_attaches_a_data_connection, hide_lemma=what_is_written_is_what_the_sender_sealed, hide_lemma=only_the_sender_resumes_a_drop]:
  "All I B gSI gSR hh kIR kRI m h2 #i #done #b.
      ResumeConfirmed(gSI, gSR, hh, m, h2) @ i & IDone(I, gSI, gSR, hh, kIR, kRI, m) @ done & Static(B, gSR) @ b
      ==> (Ex #k. Resumed(gSR, gSI, hh, m, h2) @ k & #k < #i)
        | (Ex #r. RevealStatic(gSR) @ r & #r < #done)"

end
